Advanced Haematological Malignancy
Palliative & End of Life
Refractory anemia/pancytopenia, Spontaneous bleeding, Recurrent infections

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-003
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of spontaneous mucosal bleeding and acute febrile illness in a severely immunocompromised patient.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a neutropenic patient experiencing a new, high-grade fever and sudden spontaneous gingival bleeding. Given the high risk of fatal neutropenic sepsis and catastrophic hemorrhage, urgent assessment was absolutely required to initiate emergency protocols and stabilize the patient prior to potential hospital transfer, making a delay to normal hours completely unsafe.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I've been shivering uncontrollably for hours, and my gums won't stop bleeding since I brushed my teeth."
History of Present Illness: The patient has experienced unremitting severe fatigue despite adequate rest over the last four weeks. There is a documented history of refractory symptomatic anemia requiring frequent and ongoing transfusions, alongside recurrent opportunistic infections due to profound immunosuppression. Today, the patient developed persistent, unexplained night sweats, high-grade fevers, and shivering. Two hours ago, spontaneous mucosal bleeding (gingival) and diffuse cutaneous petechiae appeared. The patient complains of intractable generalized bone pain, particularly localizing to the spine and pelvis, which has worsened with the fever. Widespread firm and painless lymphadenopathy has been noted previously, along with massive, symptomatic splenomegaly causing abdominal fullness. Rapid functional decline and worsening performance status have been evident over the past week.
Functional Status: Requires assistance with mobilizing, mostly chair-bound.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls due to severe weakness; risk of severe bleeding with minor trauma.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/55 mmHg | HR: 118 bpm | Temp: 38.9 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 95% on room air | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Pale, diaphoretic, shivering, with visible dried blood on lips and gums.
Physical Exam: Inspection reveals diffuse fresh petechiae across the lower extremities and active, slow oozing from the gingival margins. Abdominal exam confirms massive, firm splenomegaly crossing the midline. Musculoskeletal exam elicits diffuse tenderness over the lumbar spine and pelvis. The diagnostic tetrad is fulfilled by the visible spontaneous bleeding, clinical signs of recurrent infection (fever/rigors), and pallor/tachycardia reflecting the underlying refractory anemia and pancytopenia.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment. Patient is positioned near the bathroom to minimize walking distance.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking prophylactic antivirals and antifungals. No recent NSAID or anticoagulant use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Advanced Haematological Malignancy - Status: Exacerbated (Suspected Neutropenic Sepsis and Thrombocytopenia)
Differentials:

Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) - Ruled out/unlikely clinically as a primary cause without lab confirmation, though bleeding is present, there is no frank hemorrhage from multiple orifices or current hemodynamic collapse.

Viral upper respiratory infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of rhinorrhea or cough, and the profound nature of the systemic rigors and bleeding.

Medication-induced coagulopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to no current prescriptions for anticoagulants or antiplatelets.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic triad of neutropenic sepsis, profound thrombocytopenic bleeding, and severe symptomatic anemia secondary to an advanced haematological malignancy. The objective findings of a 38.9C fever, tachycardia, hypotension, and active mucosal bleeding mandate immediate intervention. Waiting for an in-hours GP appointment would expose this profoundly immunosuppressed patient to a catastrophic risk of septic shock or fatal intracranial hemorrhage. Urgent after-hours assessment was critical to confirm the severity, provide immediate advice, and orchestrate an urgent transfer to the emergency department for broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics and blood product support.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Applied topical tranexamic acid to the bleeding gingiva. Facilitated immediate emergency transfer via ambulance for suspected neutropenic sepsis.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you experience a fever or uncontrollable shivering (trigger), you must immediately notify your caregiver to seek emergency care (behavior) rather than waiting to see if it passes, ensuring life-saving antibiotics are started promptly (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A - Hospital admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Hematology team review upon arrival at the emergency department.

